  "notes": [
    "Pin 22 is an analog input with a 1.8 V ceiling, where a Raspberry Pi has GPIO25. Any HAT that drives pin 22 at 3.3 V will damage it. This is the most dangerous difference between the two headers.",
    "Power and ground land in the Raspberry Pi positions, so a HAT will power up. Everything else is Rockchip's own mapping and no Pi GPIO number means anything here.",
    "Pin names are GPIO<bank>_<group><index>. The sysfs number is bank x 32 plus group x 8 plus index, with A=0 and D=3, so GPIO4_B3 is 139. Nothing in Pi tutorials translates.",
    "The _M0, _M1, _M2 suffixes in Rockchip's documentation are mux variants: the same peripheral can appear on several pin groups, and which one is live depends on the device tree overlay. A pin listing SPI1_MOSI_M1 only does that once the matching overlay is enabled.",
    "Pins 29 and 31 differ between the ROCK 5B and the ROCK 5B+. The 5B+ has GPIO1_A3 and GPIO1_A2 there, carrying I2C4 and PWM0/1 instead.",
    "GPIO is 3.3 V with an absolute maximum of 3.63 V, so there is very little headroom. Nothing on the header is 5 V tolerant.",
    "Software support is the real cost against a Raspberry Pi. Vendor kernels, fewer overlays, and far less of the internet has hit your problem before."
  ],
